<h2>The petition</h2>
<p>Avis India Mobility Solutions approached the Delhi High Court under Section 11 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996, seeking the appointment of an arbitrator against Starion India. The parties had spent time exploring settlement; when counsel reported that no resolution could be reached, the petition was taken up for disposal. Two facts made the outcome straightforward: neither side disputed the existence of a valid arbitration clause, and both agreed that their disputes should go to arbitration.</p>

<h2>The order and its architecture</h2>
<p>The Court restated the governing discipline in one sentence: <strong>"the scope of enquiry is confined to examining the prima facie existence of an arbitration agreement."</strong> That threshold being crossed, there was, in the Court’s words, no impediment to the appointment. The operative directions follow a now-settled Delhi template:</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h4>1. Appointment</h4><p>Mr. Mukul Sinha, Advocate, was appointed sole arbitrator to adjudicate the disputes between the parties.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>2. Entering reference</h4><p>The arbitrator is to enter upon the reference within three weeks of the order.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>3. Disclosures</h4><p>Disclosures under Section 12(2) — the statement of independence and impartiality — must be made within three weeks of entering the reference.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>4. Institutional aegis</h4><p>The arbitration is to be conducted under the rules and aegis of the Delhi International Arbitration Centre, with fees per the DIAC Schedule of Fees.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>5. Pleas preserved</h4><p>All contentions — impleadment, arbitrability, jurisdiction — remain open before the tribunal; the Court’s observations bind no one on merits.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Why routing to DIAC matters</h2>
<p>The appointment is ad hoc in form — a named sole arbitrator — but institutional in operation. Placing the reference under the Delhi International Arbitration Centre’s rules gives the parties a published fee schedule, administrative case management, and procedural timelines policed by an institution rather than negotiated afresh before the tribunal. For medium-value commercial disputes, this hybrid has become the Delhi High Court’s default: the court supplies the appointment that the parties’ clause failed to produce, and the institution supplies everything else. Parties drafting arbitration clauses can achieve the same result without a court visit by designating DIAC (or another institution) as the appointing authority in the clause itself.</p>

<h2>Practice notes for petitioners and respondents</h2>
<p>For a petitioner, the case illustrates the minimum viable Section 11 file: the contract containing the clause, the invocation notice under Section 21, proof of its service, and the record of the respondent’s failure to act within thirty days. Nothing more is needed, and loading the petition with merits material only invites delay. For a respondent, the lesson is the mirror image: resistance at the Section 11 stage succeeds only on the narrow ground that no arbitration agreement exists prima facie. Objections about the scope of the clause, non-arbitrable claims, necessary parties or limitation are not wasted — they are simply banked for the tribunal, where Section 16 gives the arbitrator competence to rule on his own jurisdiction.</p>
<p>The timeline discipline in the order also deserves notice. Three weeks to enter the reference and a further defined window for Section 12(2) disclosures mean that a party can expect a functioning tribunal within roughly a month and a half of the court order. Combined with DIAC’s administrative framework, the gap between "we have a dispute" and "we have a tribunal" in Delhi commercial practice is now measured in weeks — provided the invocation paperwork was done correctly at the start.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>Settlement discussions before and during a Section 11 petition are worth attempting, but they should run on a parallel track, not as a substitute for the reference. As this order shows, the court will wait a reasonable time for negotiations — and then appoint without ceremony once they fail. A party that lets limitation run while talking settlement has traded leverage for nothing.</p>
</div>
